WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
Temporary files should use least privilege.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:09.080
Grant only the access required by the creating process and any intended consumer.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.860
If no other user or component needs the file, the default design should keep access narrow.

4
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.540
File modes and platform access controls should be reviewed together.

5
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:28.600
The creation API, requested mode, process umask, inherited permissions, and directory behavior can all affect the resulting access.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:35.260
A code review should verify the effective permission model, not only the literal mode value in one call.

7
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.580
Directory permissions matter as much as file permissions.

8
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:47.800
A restrictive file inside a poorly controlled directory may still be exposed to unwanted observation or interference through directory-level behavior.

9
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.740
The directory should match the trust assumptions of the file being created.

10
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.220
Shared-directory behavior deserves special care.

11
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:03.960
Code should validate who can create entries, who can remove entries, who can read names, and who can replace or modify content.

12
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:10.300
Those questions are especially important for service accounts, build agents, and multi-user systems.

13
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.980
Permissions are not only about secrecy.

14
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.400
They also protect integrity.

15
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.060
A temporary file that drives a later decision should not be writable by components that are not part of the intended workflow.

16
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.400
Least privilege helps ensure that temporary content remains tied to the code that owns it.
